<onlyinclude>'''Wafer testing''' checks chips while they are still attached to the silicon wafer, before they are cut apart and packaged.</onlyinclude>

'''Reading depth:''' [[Wafer testing/Simple|Simple]] · [[Wafer testing/Technical|Technical]]

A machine positions the wafer under tiny electrical contacts. A tester measures how the circuits respond. Some tests check whether the manufacturing process produced the intended electrical properties; others check each finished chip and record which ones pass.

The results form a ''wafer map'' that helps manufacturers avoid packaging known defective chips. Process-monitoring measurements may sample dedicated test structures rather than every chip. Product-die testing assigns pass/fail or performance groups (''bins''). A passing result is not a promise that the chip will never fail: packaging and later operating conditions can expose other problems.

